43
00:03:57,280 --> 00:04:00,360
what bit rates to use. And unfortunately, he made a fairly significant

44
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:06,220
error. He said, for example, that a 32K bit rate was totally adequate

45
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:09,900
for voice that it reproduced 16 kilohertz audio that it was as good

46
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:13,340
as FM. And that's not the case. Dr. Dave is confusing a couple of

47
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:17,340
different technological issues. He's confusing the bit rate of the

48
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:22,660
MP3 audio with the sample rate. The sample rate is controlled by

49
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:25,740
something called the sub nyquist theorem. The sub nyquist theorem

50
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:29,220
says that if you want to sample analog data and reproduce it

51
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:33,400
accurately, that you must sample at twice the highest frequency

52
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:36,600
you want to reproduce. So for example, if you want to reproduce

53
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:42,240
16 kilohertz audio, you need to have 32,000 samples per second. Now

54
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:46,280
in WAV file format, which is a lossless format, those are

55
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:50,600
typically 16 bit samples or two byte samples. So sampling at

56
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:56,360
32,000 samples per second is actually 64,000 bytes per

57
00:04:56,380 --> 00:05:02,060
second, which is in fact 512,000 bits per second. So that's the

58
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:05,300
sample rate, but it has relatively little to do with the bit

59
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:09,220
rate of the MP3 encoder MP3 is a lossy compression technique

60
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:12,460
similar to JPEG. We're going to throw away a lot of data when

61
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:15,860
we get these compression ratios of 2030 40 to one and so forth.

62
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:21,220
A 64K bit per second MP3 is generally considered the

63
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:26,180
equivalent of FM. It's arguable. I think that a 64K MP3

64
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:30,000
doesn't sound quite as good as good FM. I think a 96K bit per

65
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,800
second MP3 is closer to a good FM signal, but that depends on the

66
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:37,440
program material. Here's a brief history of the MP3 encoding

67
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:40,400
rates we've used here at IT Conversations. When I started

68
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:43,680
a year and a half ago, I encoded everything at 32K bits per

69
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:47,040
second. The quality wasn't good, but I still got a number of

70
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:50,160
complaints that the files were too big. The reason was that

71
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,600
the MP3 players in those days could not take very large

72
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:57,460
files. Over time things have changed quite quickly. Until

73
00:05:57,460 --> 00:06:01,180
recently we encoded everything at 48K bits per second. We got

74
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:05,620
very few complaints. But now people who have MP3 players that

75
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:09,660
have quite a bit more storage, such as a 40 gigabyte iPod, are

76
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:13,220
asking us to use higher quality bit rates. They don't mind the

77
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:16,820
larger files. So just within the past week, I've started

78
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:21,500
encoding our files at 64K bits per second. That results in

79
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:24,840
about 30 megabytes for one hour of audio. And that's my

80
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:27,760
current recommendation for what people should be using for

81
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:32,000
audio blogs, daily source code and the like. By the way, it's

82
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,560
also not true that you need a higher bit rate for music than

83
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:40,400
for voice. The waveform of human voice is very, very

84
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:43,560
complex. And in fact, you will hear distortion in the

85
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:47,320
human voice before you can detect it in most music. The

86
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:50,360
reason being that you may not know how to predict the

87
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:53,300
waveform in music, whereas with voice, if you know the

88
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:55,700
person, you have certain expectations of what their

89
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:58,900
voice will sound like. Another question that comes up is

90
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:01,700
stereo versus mono. And do you need double the bit rate

91
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:05,300
for stereo? Or are stereo files twice as large as

92
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:08,500
mono files? In wave files, they are twice as large

93
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:12,500
because it is a lossless format. But in MP3, one of the

94
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:15,060
things that you're encoding is the difference between the

95
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:17,300
left and right channels. So for example, if you're

96
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:19,900
recording voice from a single microphone, it really

97
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:24,400
doesn't matter whether you use a stereo MP3 at 64K or a

98
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:27,500
mono MP3 at 64K bits per second because they'll be

99
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,600
essentially the same. The only time that it really

100
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:33,100
matters is when you have program material where the

101
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:36,300
left and right channels are substantially different. As

102
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:39,900
to the question of VBR or variable bit rate, this

103
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:43,000
is a technique that does allow the MP3 to vary the

104
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
bit rate from one frame to the next, but it's not

105
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,340
supported by certain players. So I do not recommend

106
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:51,440
